README

This sample program uses a Raspberry Pi with two Ethernet ports as EtherCAT
master and publishes the status of the EtherCAT network to a ZeroMQ broker on
the second Ethernet port. It also receives Start/Stop commands from the
broker to request or pause the OPERATIONAL state of the bus.

Unlike sample/47-ethercatweb, the EtherCAT master runs on its own CPU core
(core 1), completely separate from the Circle scheduler, so that it is
isolated from the networking / ZeroMQ side, which runs on core 0 like all
other Circle networking code.

	+-------------------+  eth0 (raw EtherCAT frames)  +--------------+
	|  Raspberry Pi      |----------------------------->| EtherCAT     |
	|  core 1: EtherCAT  |                              | slave(s)     |
	|  (SOEM, no sched.) |                              +--------------+
	|                    |
	|  core 0: CScheduler |  eth1 (TCP/IP, ZeroMQ)      +--------------+
	|  (ZeroMQ client)   |----------------------------->| ZeroMQ       |
	+-------------------+                               | broker       |
	                                                     +--------------+

The EtherCAT master is implemented with SOEM (Simple Open EtherCAT Master) by
the OpenEtherCATsociety. SOEM is not part of Circle and has to be downloaded
before this sample can be built (see below).

Required hardware:

	* A Raspberry Pi with an on-board Ethernet controller (e.g. CM5), which
	  also supports multi-core operation (Raspberry Pi 2/3/4/5)
	* One or more EtherCAT slaves, connected to the on-board Ethernet port
	* A USB Ethernet device with a CDC Ethernet (ECM) interface, for
	  instance based on a Realtek RTL815x chip, connected to your network
	  with a DHCP server and reachable from the machine running the ZeroMQ
	  broker. On the Raspberry Pi 5 it has to be attached to a root port
	  directly, because USB 3 hubs are not supported.

Do not connect the EtherCAT port to your office network. The EtherCAT master
sends broadcast frames to this port and there is no IP stack behind it.


BUILD

This sample requires multi-core support, which is not enabled in a default
build of Circle. Reconfigure the toolchain first (repeat any options you
already used, e.g. -r for the Raspberry Pi model, --prefix, ... - see
"./configure --help"):

	./configure --multicore [your other options]

This adds "DEFINE += -DARM_ALLOW_MULTI_CORE" to Config.mk. It affects every
sample/app you build afterwards, not just this one.

Download and build the SOEM library, as described in the file
addon/soem/README:

	cd addon/soem
	git clone https://github.com/OpenEtherCATsociety/SOEM.git
	make

This also builds the OSAL patch, which makes SOEM's OSAL layer (osal.cpp)
aware of which core it is running on: CScheduler::IsActive() alone cannot
tell, because the ZeroMQ client legitimately keeps the scheduler alive on
core 0, while SOEM itself is only ever called from core 1 in this sample. If
this patch were missing, SOEM would try to use CScheduler/CMutex/CTask from
core 1, which is unsafe (see doc/multicore.txt). No change of behaviour
results for sample/47-ethercatweb, 48-elmoweb or 49-elmoplatinum, which only
ever call SOEM from core 0.

Then build this sample as usual:

	cd sample/50-ethercatzmq
	make

If the library libsoem.a has not been built, the build of this sample is
skipped with a message, so that a build of all samples does not fail.


WHAT IT DOES

* The on-board Ethernet device (net device 0, "eth0") is used by the
  EtherCAT master directly, exactly as in sample/47-ethercatweb. The class
  CEtherCATCore1Master (see ethercat_core1.h) implements the same state
  machine as CEtherCATMaster there (bus scan, CoE identity read, process
  data mapping, distributed clocks, OPERATIONAL), but its Run() method is
  called directly from CMultiCoreSupport::Run(1) (see kernel.h/.cpp) instead
  of being a CTask. It is the only code, which ever touches this net device
  or the SOEM context, so it needs no locking of its own for that; its
  status snapshot and its command slot, which are the only state shared with
  core 0, are protected with CSpinLock instead of CMutex.

* The second Ethernet device (net device 1, "eth1") runs a TCP/IP stack,
  which gets its IP address via DHCP, on core 0. CZeroMQClient (see
  zmqclient.h) is a CTask, which connects to a ZeroMQ broker and:

	- publishes a JSON status message every 500 ms (see
	  ZMQ_STATUS_INTERVAL_MS in kernel.h), with the same field names as
	  the /status.json endpoint of sample/47-ethercatweb's web server,
	- receives Start/Stop commands from the broker and forwards them to
	  the EtherCAT master (CEtherCATCore1Master::SubmitCommand()).

  A Stop command requests the SAFE_OP state (outputs stop, but the mailbox
  and distributed clocks stay alive, so the bus is fast to resume). A Start
  command re-requests OPERATIONAL. The bus is OPERATIONAL by default at
  startup, exactly as in sample/47-ethercatweb, until a Stop command is
  received.

The process data is only read by this sample. If you want to control the
outputs of a slave, extend CEtherCATCore1Master to write to the buffer
returned in the field "outputs" of the group (ctx->grouplist[0].outputs)
before the process data is sent in CyclicProcess().


THE ZEROMQ PROTOCOL

This sample hand-writes a minimal ZMTP 3.0 client (see zmtp.h/.cpp), because
there is no way to use the full libzmq library on Circle (it depends on
POSIX threads and BSD sockets, which do not exist here). The implementation
covers exactly what this sample needs and nothing more:

	* only the NULL security mechanism (no PLAIN, no CURVE),
	* only the DEALER role, talking to a ROUTER-based broker.

This means it will NOT interoperate out of the box with a plain
zmq.PUB/zmq.SUB pair or a stock XPUB/XSUB proxy broker, because those need
SUBSCRIBE/CANCEL topic-filter frames, which are not implemented. It works
with any ROUTER-based broker, which is a completely standard ZeroMQ role
(e.g. a small custom Python script, see below).

Two message "topics" are multiplexed over the one DEALER<->ROUTER
connection, distinguished by the first frame of a multipart message:

	["status",  <JSON>]	sent periodically, device -> broker
	["command", <JSON>]	received asynchronously, broker -> device,
				body is {"cmd":"start"} or {"cmd":"stop"}

The hand-written ZMTP greeting has been checked against RFC 23
(https://rfc.zeromq.org/spec/23/), but was not validated against a packet
capture of a real libzmq peer. Verify it against your actual broker (e.g.
with Wireshark's ZMTP dissector) before relying on it in an important setup.

A minimal Python test broker, which exercises this protocol end-to-end
(requires "pip install pyzmq"):

	import zmq, json

	ctx = zmq.Context()
	sock = ctx.socket(zmq.ROUTER)
	sock.bind("tcp://*:5555")

	poller = zmq.Poller()
	poller.register(sock, zmq.POLLIN)

	while True:
	    events = dict(poller.poll(200))
	    if sock in events:
	        identity, msgtype, payload = sock.recv_multipart()
	        if msgtype == b"status":
	            print(json.loads(payload))
	        # ignore other message types

	    # type "start" or "stop" + Enter on stdin (non-blocking check
	    # omitted here for brevity; use e.g. a background thread reading
	    # stdin and calling sock.send_multipart(
	    #     [identity, b"command", json.dumps({"cmd": cmd}).encode()])


CONFIGURATION

The used net devices, the cycle time and the ZeroMQ broker address are
defined in kernel.h:

	ETHERCAT_NET_DEVICE		net device for EtherCAT (default 0)
	ZMQ_NET_DEVICE			net device for the ZeroMQ client (default 1)
	ETHERCAT_CYCLE_TIME_US		process data cycle time (default 4000 us)
	ZMQ_BROKER_HOST_DEFAULT	default ZeroMQ broker host
	ZMQ_BROKER_PORT_DEFAULT	default ZeroMQ broker port (default 5555)
	ZMQ_STATUS_INTERVAL_MS		interval of the status messages (default 500 ms)

The on-board Ethernet device is initialized before the USB host controller in
CKernel::Initialize(), so that it becomes net device 0 and the USB Ethernet
device becomes net device 1. See doc/multiple-net-devices.txt for details.

The broker address can be overridden without rebuilding, using these options
in cmdline.txt on the SD card:

	zmqbroker=192.168.1.10		ZeroMQ broker host name or IP address
	zmqport=5555			ZeroMQ broker port

The number of slaves kept in the status and the size of the process data
image are defined in ethercat_core1.h (ETHERCAT_MAX_SLAVES and
ETHERCAT_IOMAP_SIZE). The maximum number of slaves on the bus is defined as
EC_MAXSLAVE in addon/soem/include/soem/ec_options.h.


DEBUG OUTPUT

The debug messages (the Circle system log) are written to the serial
interface, so that the application can be troubleshooted, while it is
running. On the Raspberry Pi 5 the dedicated 3-pin UART connector is used,
served by uart10 (serial device 10 in Circle, "ttyS11"). Other Raspberry Pi
models use the UART on GPIO14/15. Settings: 115200 baud, 8 data bits, no
parity, 1 stop bit, defined in kernel.h (SERIAL_DEVICE_NUMBER and
SERIAL_BAUD_RATE).

The following options can be added to cmdline.txt on the SD card:

	loglevel=4	log level: 0 (panic) to 4 (debug, this is the default)
	logtarget=tty1	write the log messages to the screen (HDMI) instead
	logtarget=ttyS11 write them to the UART connector (this is the default)

The output looks like this:

	00:00:02.14 kernel: Compile time: Aug 19 2026 09:12:33
	00:00:02.14 kernel: 2 net device(s) available
	00:00:02.15 ethercat: EtherCAT master starting on eth0 (cycle time 4000 us)
	00:00:02.31 ethercat: 3 slave(s) found
	00:00:03.02 ethercat: Process data mapped: 1 byte(s) out, 2 byte(s) in, expected WKC 5
	00:00:03.24 ethercat: All slaves are operational
	00:00:04.01 zmqclient: Connected to broker 192.168.1.10:5555
	00:00:12.15 kernel: EtherCAT: OPERATIONAL, 3 slave(s), WKC 5 of 5, 2412 cycle(s)

Sending {"cmd":"stop"} from the test broker to the device (as the "command"
message described above) shows:

	00:01:00.00 ethercat: Stop requested, transitioning to SAFE_OP

...and the "state" field of the next status message changes from
"OPERATIONAL" to "Stopped". Sending {"cmd":"start"} shows the reverse
transition and "state" goes back to "OPERATIONAL".

To see the internal debug messages of SOEM too, build the SOEM library with:

	cd addon/soem
	make clean
	make EC_DEBUG=1

These messages are written with the log level LogDebug and are very verbose.


NOTES

Core 1 runs the EtherCAT cyclic loop directly, without the Circle scheduler:
it uses CTimer::SimpleusDelay() to pace the cycle instead of
CScheduler::usSleep(), and it is the exclusive owner of the SOEM context, the
on-board net device and the process data image, so it needs no locking for
those. Only its status snapshot (read by the ZeroMQ client) and its command
slot (written by the ZeroMQ client) are shared across cores, and both are
protected with CSpinLock. Do not add any other cross-core sharing to
CEtherCATCore1Master without the same care: CScheduler, CTask, CMutex, and
the CSocket/CNetSubSystem based networking stack are only safe on core 0
(see doc/multicore.txt).

Cycle times below one millisecond are not recommended, same as for
sample/47-ethercatweb. There is no synchronization of the system time of the
Raspberry Pi to the distributed clock of the EtherCAT network.

If no slaves are found or the Ethernet link is down, the master scans the
bus again every five seconds; a pending Stop command is remembered across a
rescan, so the bus stays in SAFE_OP, once it reconnects. If the connection to
the broker is lost, the ZeroMQ client retries every five seconds.

A Stop command targets SAFE_OP, not INIT, so it does not fully de-energize
the slave outputs or drop the mailbox/CoE context (which would need a full
CEtherCATCore1Master::Configure() to resume). If your slave hardware needs a
harder stop, extend HandleCommand() in ethercat_core1.cpp accordingly.
